Chamfers: a chamfer as wide as a ledge along a wall's line and arc
(mitcad#263).

The case: a wall standing on a ledge as wide as the chamfer, the wall's
foot a line and a quarter arc meeting tangentially; the foot's concave
edges chamfered, so that the chamfer's contact on the ledge runs along the
ledge's outer edges and the ledge's top is consumed (0140).

- ChFi3d_Builder_SpKP's HatchOnBoundary took a moved line as lying on the
  face's boundary only when all its samples were ON. Along the arc, the
  contact line runs on the ledge's outer edge and, at the junction with the
  line, inside the face where the hatcher left the part along the edge out:
  the arc's piece was hatched to a point. A domain is now on the boundary
  when none of its samples is OUT and at least two are ON (or a closed line
  lies on the boundary all round, as before).
- ChFi3d_CutConsumedFaces accepted a consumed face's strip only when its
  vertices were the spine's or those of the obstacle edge of its own
  SurfData. The ledge's top lies along both pieces' obstacle edges (the
  line's and the arc's): it kept its strip and was not consumed. The
  vertices of every obstacle edge of the stripe's side are now accepted.
- ChFi3d_ContactsAlongFaceEdges: a contact along a whole open edge (not a
  part) left its ends, not on an arc, as points near the edge's vertices,
  and the line's and the arc's pieces met at two distinct vertices. Such an
  end within tolerance of a vertex of the edge is now that vertex.

Before, the chamfer failed, or gave a shape with self-intersecting wires.

